{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a43c39f9649c92c1","type":"tool_result","content":"300\t<div id=\"S2.SS1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n301\t<p id=\"S2.SS1.p1.3\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S2.SS1.p1.3.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">BEATs</span> <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib16\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">16</a>]</cite> is an audio <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> model widely adopted for <abbr title=\"anomalous sound detection\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">ASD</span></abbr> <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib9\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">9</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib10\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">10</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib11\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">11</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib12\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">12</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib13\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">13</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib8\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">8</a>]</cite>. It performs masked-prediction-based <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> in a discrete token space.\n302\tA key feature of BEATs is its iterative training pipeline, in which the acoustic tokenizer and the <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> model are alternately refined through knowledge distillation.\n303\t<span id=\"S2.SS1.p1.3.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">EAT</span> <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib17\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">17</a>]</cite> has also demonstrated strong <abbr title=\"anomalous sound detection\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">ASD</span></abbr> performance <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib10\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">10</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib11\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">11</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib8\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">8</a>]</cite>. It is based on a masked bootstrapping framework, in which a student model is trained to reconstruct the latent representations of a teacher model at masked positions.\n304\tIt also introduces utterance-level losses and a CLS token to capture global information.\n305\t<span id=\"S2.SS1.p1.3.3\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Dasheng</span> <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib18\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">18</a>]</cite> is a recent <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> model based on a masked autoencoder framework and is trained on 272k hours of diverse audio data.\n306\tDasheng takes mel-spectrogram segments as input, whereas BEATs and EAT take mel-spectrogram patches as input.\n307\tThese models convert the input sequence into a representation sequence <math id=\"S2.SS1.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{r}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{r}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id=\"S2.SS1.p1.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"L\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of mel-spectrogram patches or segments and <math id=\"S2.SS1.p1.3.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"D\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the representation dimension.\n308\tThe representation is then either directly used in the backend or further fine-tuned on machine sound datasets.</p>\n309\t</div>\n310\t</section>\n311\t<section id=\"S2.SS2\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n312\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n313\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">2.2 </span><span id=\"S2.SS2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Discriminative fine-tuning</span>\n314\t</h3>\n315\t\n316\t<div id=\"S2.SS2.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n317\t<p id=\"S2.SS2.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Fine-tuning is performed based on a classification task using machine-information labels <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib12\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">12</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib13\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">13</a>]</cite>.\n318\tIn recent DCASE datasets <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib5\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">5</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib6\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">6</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib7\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">7</a>]</cite>, the machine-information labels in the training data include the machine type (e.g., bearing), domain (source or target), and machine-dependent fine-grained attribute labels (e.g., rotation speed).\n319\tHere, different attributes are included in the source and target domains.\n320\tAlso, some machine types do not have attribute labels.\n321\tFor such machine types, pseudo-labeling is employed <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib19\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">19</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib13\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">13</a>]</cite>.\n322\tPseudo attribute labels are generated by applying clustering to the representations of the training data.\n323\tThe pseudo attribute labels are then combined with the other available coarse labels of the target machine (i.e., the machine-type and domain labels) and the attribute labels of the other machines to form a multi-class classification task.</p>\n324\t</div>\n325\t</section>\n326\t<section id=\"S2.SS3\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n327\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n328\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">2.3 </span><span id=\"S2.SS3.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Backend techniques</span>\n329\t</h3>\n330\t\n331\t<div id=\"S2.SS3.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n332\t<p id=\"S2.SS3.p1.6\" class=\"ltx_p\">In the backend, the <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> representation sequence <math id=\"S2.SS3.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{r}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is aggregated to calculate the anomaly score.\n333\tSaengthong et al. <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib9\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">9</a>]</cite> proposed an aggregation technique that utilizes frequency information.\n334\tThis technique reshapes <math id=\"S2.SS3.p1.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{r}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{r}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id=\"S2.SS3.p1.3.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{r}_{\\rm TF}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{T\\times F\\times D}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>TF</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{r}_{\\rm TF}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{T\\times F\\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id=\"S2.SS3.p1.4.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"T\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id=\"S2.SS3.p1.5.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"F\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math> denote the numbers of mel-spectrogram patches along the time and frequency axes, respectively.\n335\tIt then applies average pooling along the time axis and concatenates the frequency axis to obtain a single <math id=\"S2.SS3.p1.6.m6\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"FD\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">FD</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional frequency-preserving representation.\n336\tThis representation was shown to improve backend performance compared with a global representation averaged over both the time and frequency axes <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib9\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">9</a>]</cite>.</p>\n337\t</div>\n338\t<div id=\"S2.SS3.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n339\t<p id=\"S2.SS3.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">BEAM <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib10\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">10</a>]</cite> is another technique that builds on a frequency-preserving representation.\n340\tIt constructs a <math id=\"S2.SS3.p2.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"D\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional representation memory bank for each frequency index.\n341\tAn anomaly score is calculated separately for each frequency index and then averaged across the frequency indices to obtain a sample-level anomaly score.\n342\tThis technique mitigates undesirably high anomaly scores caused by variations within normal sounds.\n343\tFor example, an unseen combination of machine sound and noise can yield a high anomaly score, even when each frequency component has been separately observed in the training data.\n344\tBy avoiding strict joint comparison across frequencies, this technique can improve robustness to normal variations while retaining sensitivity to anomalies in each frequency.</p>\n345\t</div>\n346\t<div id=\"S2.SS3.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n347\t<span id=\"S2.SS3.p3.6\" class=\"ltx_ERROR undefined\">\\Ac</span>\n348\t<p id=\"S2.SS3.p3.5\" class=\"ltx_p\">rdp <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib11\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">11</a>]</cite> is a pooling technique designed as an alternative to simple average pooling.\n349\tIt computes a weighted average of the representation sequence, assigning each representation <math id=\"S2.SS3.p3.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{r}_{l}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{r}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> a weight <math id=\"S2.SS3.p3.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"w_{l}={(1+\\hat{d}_{l})^{\\gamma}}/{\\sum_{l^{\\prime}}(1+\\hat{d}_{l^{\\prime}})^{\\gamma}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>d</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo rspace=\"0.055em\">/</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace=\"0em\">∑</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>d</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{l}={(1+\\hat{d}_{l})^{\\gamma}}/{\\sum_{l^{\\prime}}(1+\\hat{d}_{l^{\\prime}})^{\\gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id=\"S2.SS3.p3.3.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\hat{d}_{l}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>d</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{d}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Euclidean distance between <math id=\"S2.SS3.p3.4.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{r}_{l}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{r}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> and the sequence average, normalized by the maximum such distance within the sequence, and <math id=\"S2.SS3.p3.5.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\gamma\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter.\n350\tThis emphasizes representations that deviate from the average, thereby preserving temporally localized anomalies that are smoothed out by typical average pooling.</p>\n351\t</div>\n352\t</section>\n353\t</section>\n354\t<section id=\"S3\" class=\"ltx_section\">\n355\t<h2 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_section\">\n356\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section\">3 </span><span id=\"S3.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps\">Proposed method</span>\n357\t</h2>\n358\t\n359\t<div id=\"S3.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n360\t<p id=\"S3.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We introduce <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr> models to the <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-ASD</span></abbr> task.\n361\tWe follow the training procedure of the previous NA-BEATs implementation <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib15\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">15</a>]</cite> but modify the auxiliary noise condition to match the <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-ASD</span></abbr> task.\n362\tAlso, we design multiple <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr> models.\n363\tSpecifically, we design NA-BEATs, NA-EAT, and NA-Dasheng in the same manner based on BEATs (<span id=\"S3.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">BEATs_iter3.pt</span>), EAT (<span id=\"S3.p1.1.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">EAT-base_epoch30_pretrain</span>), and Dasheng (<span id=\"S3.p1.1.3\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">dasheng-base</span>), respectively.</p>\n364\t</div>\n365\t<section id=\"S3.SS1\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n366\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n367\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">3.1 </span><span id=\"S3.SS1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Noise-aware self-supervised learning (NA-SSL)</span>\n368\t</h3>\n369\t\n370\t<div id=\"S3.SS1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n371\t<p id=\"S3.SS1.p1.2\" class=\"ltx_p\">Following <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib15\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">15</a>]</cite>, we train an <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr> model by distillation with a  <span title=\"mean squared error\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_long\">mean squared error</span></span> (<abbr title=\"mean squared error\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">MSE</span></abbr>) loss between the clean target representation sequence <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{r}\\!\\in\\!\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo lspace=\"0.108em\" rspace=\"0.108em\">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{r}\\!\\in\\!\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> from the frozen original <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> model and the estimated representation sequence <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\hat{\\mathbf{r}}\\!\\in\\!\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace=\"0.108em\" rspace=\"0.108em\">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{\\mathbf{r}}\\!\\in\\!\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib15\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">15</a>]</cite>, given by</p>\n372\t<table id=\"S5.EGx1\" class=\"ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table\">\n373\t\n374\t<tbody id=\"S3.E1\"><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n375\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n376\t<td class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell\"><math id=\"S3.E1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\displaystyle\\mathbf{r}\\!\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\displaystyle\\mathbf{r}\\!</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n377\t<td class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell\"><math id=\"S3.E1.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\displaystyle=\\!\\mathrm{SSL}(\\mathbf{s}),\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi></mi><mo rspace=\"0.108em\">=</mo><mrow><mi>SSL</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\displaystyle=\\!\\mathrm{SSL}(\\mathbf{s}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n378\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n379\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(1)</span></td>\n380\t</tr></tbody>\n381\t<tbody id=\"S3.E2\"><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n382\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n383\t<td class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell\"><math id=\"S3.E2.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\displaystyle\\hat{\\mathbf{r}}\\!\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\displaystyle\\hat{\\mathbf{r}}\\!</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n384\t<td class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell\"><math id=\"S3.E2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\displaystyle=\\!\\mathrm{NASSL}({\\mathbf{x}},\\mathbf{n}^{\\prime}),\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi></mi><mo rspace=\"0.108em\">=</mo><mrow><mi>NASSL</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\displaystyle=\\!\\mathrm{NASSL}({\\mathbf{x}},\\mathbf{n}^{\\prime}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n385\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n386\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(2)</span></td>\n387\t</tr></tbody>\n388\t</table>\n389\t<p id=\"S3.SS1.p1.8\" class=\"ltx_p\">where <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.3.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{s}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.4.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{x}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.5.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{n}^{\\prime}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{n}^{\\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denote clean target sound, noisy target sound, and auxiliary noise signal, respectively.\n390\tFor the <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-ASD</span></abbr> task, we use the close-microphone signal as <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.6.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{x}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and the far-microphone signal as <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.7.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{n}^{\\prime}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{n}^{\\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.\n391\tWe define the clean target sound <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.8.m6\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{s}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math> as the signal obtained at the close microphone using a room impulse response including early reflections within 50 ms.\n392\tFor NA-EAT, which produces a CLS token in addition to the representation sequence, we apply the <abbr title=\"mean squared error\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">MSE</span></abbr> loss to both outputs with equal weights.</p>\n393\t</div>\n394\t<div id=\"S3.SS1.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n395\t<p id=\"S3.SS1.p2.4\" class=\"ltx_p\">For all base <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> models, we construct NA extensions by inserting trainable NA layers into the frozen original <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> model after each Transformer layer, as in <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib15\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">15</a>]</cite>.\n396\tHere, BEATs, EAT, and Dasheng all have a projection layer and 12 Transformer layers.\n397\tBoth the close-microphone signal <math id=\"S3.SS1.p2.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{x}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and the far-microphone signal <math id=\"S3.SS1.p2.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{n}^{\\prime}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{n}^{\\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are passed through the original base <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> model.\n398\tAfter each original Transformer layer, an NA layer refines the noisy target representation <math id=\"S3.SS1.p2.3.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}}\\!\\in\\!\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0.108em\" rspace=\"0.108em\">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}}\\!\\in\\!\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> using the corresponding noise representation <math id=\"S3.SS1.p2.4.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{n}^{\\prime}}\\!\\in\\!\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><msup><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace=\"0.108em\" rspace=\"0.108em\">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{n}^{\\prime}}\\!\\in\\!\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>.\n399\tSpecifically, the cross-attention-based NA layer works as follows <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib15\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">15</a>]</cite>:</p>\n400\t<table id=\"S5.EGx2\" class=\"ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table\">\n401\t\n402\t<tbody id=\"S3.E3\"><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n403\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n404\t<td class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell\"><math id=\"S3.E3.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\displaystyle\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\displaystyle\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n405\t<td class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell\"><math id=\"S3.E3.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\displaystyle\\xleftarrow{}\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}}+\\mathrm{MHCA}(\\mathrm{RMSNorm}(\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}}),\\mathrm{RMSNorm}(\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{n}^{\\prime}})),\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi></mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mo>←</mo><mi></mi></mover><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>MHCA</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>RMSNorm</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>RMSNorm</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><msup><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\displaystyle\\xleftarrow{}\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}}+\\mathrm{MHCA}(\\mathrm{RMSNorm}(\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}}),\\mathrm{RMSNorm}(\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{n}^{\\prime}})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n406\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n407\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(3)</span></td>\n408\t</tr></tbody>\n409\t<tbody id=\"S3.E4\"><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n410\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n411\t<td class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell\"><math id=\"S3.E4.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\displaystyle\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\displaystyle\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n412\t<td class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell\"><math id=\"S3.E4.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\displaystyle\\xleftarrow{}\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}}+\\mathrm{FFN}_{\\mathrm{SwiGLU}}(\\mathrm{RMSNorm}(\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}})),\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi></mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mo>←</mo><mi></mi></mover><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>FFN</mi><mi>SwiGLU</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>RMSNorm</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\displaystyle\\xleftarrow{}\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}}+\\mathrm{FFN}_{\\mathrm{SwiGLU}}(\\mathrm{RMSNorm}(\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n413\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n414\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(4)</span></td>\n415\t</tr></tbody>\n416\t</table>\n417\t<p id=\"S3.SS1.p2.7\" class=\"ltx_p\">where <math id=\"S3.SS1.p2.5.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathrm{MHCA}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>MHCA</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{MHCA}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes multi-head cross-attention <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib20\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">20</a>]</cite>, with the first argument used as the query and the second argument used as the key and value.\n418\t<math id=\"S3.SS1.p2.6.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathrm{RMSNorm}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>RMSNorm</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{RMSNorm}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes root mean square normalization <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib21\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">21</a>]</cite>, and <math id=\"S3.SS1.p2.7.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathrm{FFN}_{\\mathrm{SwiGLU}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>FFN</mi><mi>SwiGLU</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{FFN}_{\\mathrm{SwiGLU}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a  <span title=\"Swish gated linear unit\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_long\">Swish gated linear unit</span></span> (<abbr title=\"Swish gated linear unit\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SwiGLU</span></abbr>)-based  <span title=\"feed-forward network\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_long\">feed-forward network</span></span> (<abbr title=\"feed-forward network\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">FFN</span></abbr>) <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib22\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">22</a>]</cite>.\n419\tThe NA layer incorporates auxiliary noise information to refine the noisy representation and was shown to improve performance under noisy conditions in <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib15\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">15</a>]</cite>.</p>\n420\t</div>\n421\t</section>\n422\t<section id=\"S3.SS2\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n423\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n424\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">3.2 </span><span id=\"S3.SS2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Simulation of two-channel recordings</span>\n425\t</h3>\n426\t\n427\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n428\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">For training the <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr> models, we simulate two-channel recordings using diverse audio signals.\n429\tFSD50K <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib23\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">23</a>]</cite> is used as the target-sound dataset, while WHAM!48kHz <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib24\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">24</a>]</cite>, DEMAND <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib25\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">25</a>]</cite>, and QUT-NOISE <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib26\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">26</a>]</cite> are used as the noise datasets.\n430\tAll signals are resampled to 16 kHz and randomly cropped or zero-padded to 10 s.</p>\n431\t</div>\n432\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n433\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We simulate room impulse responses using Pyroomacoustics <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib27\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">27</a>]</cite>, as shown in <a href=\"#S3.F2\" title=\"In 3.3 ASD procedure using NA-SSL models ‣ 3 Proposed method ‣ Anomalous Sound Detection Meets Noise-Aware Self-Supervised Learning\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">Fig.</span> <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">2</span></a>.\n434\tThe reverberation time is randomly sampled from [0.1, 0.4] s.\n435\tThe target sound source is randomly placed with a 0.5 m margin from the walls.\n436\tThe close microphone is fixed 0.05 m from the target source, while the far microphone is randomly placed [0.1, 1.0] m away.\n437\tFour different noise signals are randomly selected and played, each placed 0.2 m away from a corner of the room.\n438\tThe SNR at the close microphone is randomly selected from [-10, 10] dB.</p>\n439\t</div>\n440\t</section>\n441\t<section id=\"S3.SS3\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n442\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n443\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">3.3 </span><span id=\"S3.SS3.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">ASD procedure using NA-SSL models</span>\n444\t</h3>\n445\t\n446\t<div id=\"S3.SS3.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n447\t<span id=\"S3.SS3.p1.2\" class=\"ltx_ERROR undefined\">\\Ac</span>\n448\t<p id=\"S3.SS3.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">nassl models can be combined with well-established <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr>-based <abbr title=\"anomalous sound detection\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">ASD</span></abbr> techniques described in <a href=\"#S2\" title=\"2 SSL-based ASD ‣ Anomalous Sound Detection Meets Noise-Aware Self-Supervised Learning\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">Section</span> <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">2</span></a>.\n449\tFor the <abbr title=\"anomalous sound detection\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">ASD</span></abbr> backend, we simply use the estimated clean representation <math id=\"S3.SS3.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\hat{\\mathbf{r}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{\\mathbf{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of the noisy representation.\n450\tSince the output of an <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr> model has the same format as that of the original <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> model, any backend technique designed for <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> representations can be directly applied to <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr> representations.</p>\n451\t</div>\n452\t<div id=\"S3.SS3.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n453\t<p id=\"S3.SS3.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We can also perform discriminative fine-tuning of <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr> on top of <math id=\"S3.SS3.p2.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\hat{\\mathbf{r}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{\\mathbf{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where both the <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> model and the NA layers are updated.\n454\tWhile the base <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> model is shared between the close- and far-microphone signals, the NA layers refine only the close-microphone representation.\n455\tTherefore, fine-tuning the NA layers allows them to adapt specifically to the close-microphone representation, which predominantly contains the target machine sound.</p>\n456\t</div>\n457\t<figure id=\"S3.F2\" class=\"ltx_figure\"><span class=\"ltx_inline-block\"><svg id=\"S3.F2.pic1\" class=\"ltx_picture ltx_centering\" height=\"156.52\" overflow=\"visible\" version=\"1.1\" viewBox=\"0 0 285.44 156.52\" width=\"285.44\"><g transform=\"translate(0,156.52) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(0.48,0) translate(0,26.9)\"><g style=\"--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#F7A3A8;\" fill=\"#F7A3A8\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.7pt\"><path d=\"M 0 0 M 0 0 L 0 129.13 L 258.27 129.13 L 258.27 0 Z M 258.27 129.13\"></path></g><g style=\"--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.7pt\"><path d=\"M 22.6 22.6 M 22.6 22.6 L 22.6 106.54 L 235.67 106.54 L 235.67 22.6 Z M 235.67 106.54\"></path></g><g style=\"--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;\" stroke=\"#000000\" fill=\"#000000\"><g style=\"--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.7pt\"><path style=\"fill:none\" d=\"M 22.6 22.6 M 22.6 22.6 L 22.6 106.54 L 235.67 106.54 L 235.67 22.6 Z M 235.67 106.54\"></path></g><g style=\"--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.45pt\"><path style=\"fill:none\" d=\"M 12.91 12.91 M 12.91 12.91 L 12.91 116.22 L 245.35 116.22 L 245.35 12.91 Z M 245.35 116.22\"></path></g><g style=\"--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.55pt\"><path styl