0%;width:19.9pt;\">(3.03)</span>\n760\t</td>\n761\t<td id=\"S3.T1.8.4.17.8\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb\">\n762\t<span id=\"S3.T1.8.4.17.8.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">90.03</span> <span id=\"S3.T1.8.4.17.8.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_align_right ltx_inline-block\" style=\"font-size:70%;width:23.9pt;\">(2.65)</span>\n763\t</td>\n764\t<td id=\"S3.T1.8.4.17.9\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb\">\n765\t<span id=\"S3.T1.8.4.17.9.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">61.93</span> <span id=\"S3.T1.8.4.17.9.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_align_right ltx_inline-block\" style=\"font-size:70%;width:19.9pt;\">(2.13)</span>\n766\t</td>\n767\t<td id=\"S3.T1.8.4.17.10\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb\"><span id=\"S3.T1.8.4.17.10.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">63.10</span></td>\n768\t</tr>\n769\t</table>\n770\t</span></div>\n771\t</figure>\n772\t</section>\n773\t</section>\n774\t<section id=\"S4\" class=\"ltx_section\">\n775\t<h2 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_section\">\n776\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section\">4 </span><span id=\"S4.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps\">Experimental evaluation</span>\n777\t</h2>\n778\t\n779\t<div id=\"S4.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n780\t<p id=\"S4.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">In the experimental evaluation, we demonstrate the general effectiveness of the <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr> framework across diverse settings, including three base <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> models, multiple backend techniques, and configurations with and without discriminative fine-tuning.</p>\n781\t</div>\n782\t<section id=\"S4.SS1\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n783\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n784\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">4.1 </span><span id=\"S4.SS1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Setup</span>\n785\t</h3>\n786\t\n787\t<div id=\"S4.SS1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n788\t<p id=\"S4.SS1.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We conducted experimental evaluations using the DCASE 2026 Challenge Task 2 dataset, compiled from ToyADMOS2 <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib28\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">28</a>]</cite> and MIMII DG <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib29\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">29</a>]</cite>.\n789\tThe dataset consists of development and evaluation sets with distinct machine types.\n790\tThe development set includes seven machine types: bearingEmu, fan, gearboxEmu, valveEmu, sliderEmu, ToyCarEmu, and ToyCar.\n791\tThe evaluation set includes five machine types: ToyDrone, ToothBrush, SewingMachine, Sander, and BlowerDustCollector.\n792\tEach machine type has 1000 training samples and 200 test samples.\n793\tAll training samples are normal, with 99% from the source domain and 1% from the target domain.\n794\tAttribute labels are available only for the training data of the following machine types: fan, gearboxEmu, ToyCarEmu, ToyDrone, Sander, and BlowerDustCollector.\n795\tThe test data in the development set consist of 50 samples for each combination of domain and normal/anomalous class.\n796\tThe test data in the evaluation set are provided without domain and normal/anomalous labels for the official challenge evaluation.\n797\tEach recording is a 6–16-second two-channel signal sampled at 16 kHz.</p>\n798\t</div>\n799\t<div id=\"S4.SS1.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n800\t<p id=\"S4.SS1.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">For the NA layers, we set the number of heads in MHCA to 8 and the hidden size of the <abbr title=\"feed-forward network\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">FFN</span></abbr> to 2304.\n801\tWe trained the <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr> models for 200 epochs using the AdamW optimizer, a fixed learning rate of 0.0001, and a batch size of 160.\n802\tWe applied  <span title=\"exponential moving average\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_long\">exponential moving average</span></span> (<abbr title=\"exponential moving average\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">EMA</span></abbr>) with a decay rate of 0.999 after 20k training steps.</p>\n803\t</div>\n804\t<div id=\"S4.SS1.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n805\t<p id=\"S4.SS1.p3.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">For discriminative fine-tuning, we generated pseudo labels for machine types without attribute labels.\n806\tWhen fine-tuning a given <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> or <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr> model, we generated the pseudo labels from that same model.\n807\tThe pseudo labels were generated by applying k-means clustering to the frequency-preserving representation.\n808\tFor each machine type, pseudo labels were generated in the source domain, where the number of clusters was set to 0.8% of the number of samples.\n809\tFor the target domain, all samples were assigned to a single target-domain class within each machine type.</p>\n810\t</div>\n811\t<div id=\"S4.SS1.p4\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n812\t<p id=\"S4.SS1.p4.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">During the discriminative fine-tuning, we aggregated the representation sequence <math id=\"S4.SS1.p4.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{r}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> into a single 256-dimensional discriminative feature using an attentive statistics pooling layer <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib30\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">30</a>]</cite> and a linear layer.\n813\tIn the backend, the <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> representation sequence before aggregation was used rather than the resulting discriminative feature.\n814\tFor the fine-tuning of the original <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> models, we applied  <span title=\"low-rank adaptation\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_long\">low-rank adaptation</span></span> (<abbr title=\"low-rank adaptation\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">LoRA</span></abbr>) with a rank of 64 to the query, key, and value projection layers in the attention modules.\n815\tIn <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr>, <abbr title=\"low-rank adaptation\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">LoRA</span></abbr> was also applied to MHCA in the NA layers with the same configuration.\n816\tWe fine-tuned the models for 25 epochs using sub-cluster AdaCos <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib31\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">31</a>]</cite> with 16 sub-clusters, mixup with a probability of 0.5, AdamW optimizer, and a batch size of 8.\n817\tThe learning rate was linearly increased from 0 to 0.0001 over the first 5,000 steps.</p>\n818\t</div>\n819\t<div id=\"S4.SS1.p5\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n820\t<p id=\"S4.SS1.p5.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We evaluated the performance with multiple backend techniques: frequency-preserving representation, BEAM, and <abbr title=\"relative deviation pooling\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">RDP</span></abbr>.\n821\tTo address the data imbalance between the source and target domains, we always used variance-minimization-based score rescaling <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib32\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">32</a>]</cite> with four nearest neighbors.\n822\tCosine distance was used to calculate the anomaly score.</p>\n823\t</div>\n824\t<div id=\"S4.SS1.p6\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n825\t<p id=\"S4.SS1.p6.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">As the evaluation metric, we used the official score <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib7\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">7</a>]</cite> calculated as the harmonic mean of three types of <abbr title=\"area under the   () curve\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">AUC</span></abbr>: (a)  <span title=\"partial   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_long\">partial <abbr title=\"\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"></abbr></span></span> (<abbr title=\"partial   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">pAUC</span></abbr>) with <math id=\"S4.SS1.p6.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"p=0.1\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, calculated using samples from both the source and target domains; and (b) source-domain and (c) target-domain <abbr title=\"area under the   () curve\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short-plural\">AUCs</span></abbr>, each calculated using normal samples from the corresponding domain and anomalous samples from both domains.\n826\tWe performed <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr> pre-training and discriminative fine-tuning for three trials with different random seeds.\n827\tWe report the mean and 95% confidence interval (CI95) of the official scores across the three trials and also report the ensemble result obtained by averaging the anomaly scores across the trials.</p>\n828\t</div>\n829\t<figure id=\"S4.T2\" class=\"ltx_table\">\n830\t<figcaption class=\"ltx_caption ltx_centering\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_table\">Table 2: </span>\n831\tEvaluation using multiple backends.\n832\tThe values are harmonic mean of the official scores over machine types in the development dataset.\n833\tThe values are reported as “Mean (CI95)”.\n834\tNA denotes <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr> pre-training, and Dis denotes discriminative fine-tuning.\n835\tGlobal and Freq denote global and frequency-preserving representations, respectively.\n836\tAP denotes the average pooling.\n837\tThe value in parentheses after “RDP” indicates <math id=\"S4.T2.2.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\gamma\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.\n838\tSince Dasheng extracts segment-wise representations, the frequency-preserving representation is identical to the global representation.\n839\t</figcaption>\n840\t<div id=\"S4.T2.3\" class=\"ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer\" style=\"width:433.6pt;height:314.2pt;vertical-align:-154.4pt;\"><span class=\"ltx_transformed_inner\" style=\"transform:translate(16.3pt,-11.8pt) scale(1.08108305518192,1.08108305518192) ;\">\n841\t<table id=\"S4.T2.3.1\" class=\"ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle\">\n842\t<tr id=\"S4.T2.3.1.1\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n843\t<td id=\"S4.T2.3.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt\">\n844\t<span id=\"S4.T2.3.1.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text\"></span> <span id=\"S4.T2.3.1.1.1.2\" class=\"ltx_text\">\n845\t<span id=\"S4.T2.3.1.1.1.2.1\" class=\"ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle\">\n846\t<span id=\"S4.T2.3.1.1.1.2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n847\t<span id=\"S4.T2.3.1.1.1.2.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center\">Base</span></span>\n848\t</span></span><span id=\"S4.T2.3.1.1.1.3\" class=\"ltx_