=\"S4.T2.3.1.13.6.2.1.2\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n<span id=\"S4.T2.3.1.13.6.2.1.2.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center\"><span id=\"S4.T2.3.1.13.6.2.1.2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"font-size:70%;\">(1.89)</span></span></span>\n</span></span><span id=\"S4.T2.3.1.13.6.3\" class=\"ltx_text\"></span></td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n</span></div>\n</figure>\n</section>\n<section id=\"S4.SS2\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">4.2 </span><span id=\"S4.SS2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Results</span>\n</h3>\n\n<div id=\"S4.SS2.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n<p id=\"S4.SS2.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><a href=\"#S3.T1\" title=\"In 3.3 ASD procedure using NA-SSL models ‣ 3 Proposed method ‣ Anomalous Sound Detection Meets Noise-Aware Self-Supervised Learning\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">Table</span> <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">1</span></a> compares frontends using the same backend consisting of BEAM and <abbr title=\"relative deviation pooling\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">RDP</span></abbr> with <math id=\"S4.SS2.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\gamma=4\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=4</annotation></semantics></math>.\nFirst, we can see that <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr> is effective regardless of the base model and whether discriminative fine-tuning is applied.\n<abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr> yields substantial improvements of over 20% for valveEmu and also improves performance for the other machine types.\nDiscriminative fine-tuning further improves performance and achieves the best performance for all base models.\nAlso, by using pseudo labels, the benefits of discriminative fine-tuning can be observed even for machine types without attribute labels.\nWe also observe high variance in <abbr title=\"anomalous sound detection\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">ASD</span></abbr> performance for <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr> models, highlighting the importance of ensembling multiple trials.</p>\n</div>\n<div id=\"S4.SS2.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n<p id=\"S4.SS2.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><a href=\"#S4.T2\" title=\"In 4.1 Setup ‣ 4 Experimental evaluation ‣ Anomalous Sound Detection Meets Noise-Aware Self-Supervised Learning\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">Table</span> <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">2</span></a> shows the results obtained using different backend techniques.\nThe results demonstrate the effectiveness of the individual backend techniques and show that their benefits also carry over to <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr>-based frontends.\nIn particular, we can see that BEAM substantially improves performance.\nFor <abbr title=\"relative deviation pooling\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">RDP</span></abbr>, larger values of <math id=\"S4.SS2.p2.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\gamma\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> yield greater improvements for BEATs and Dasheng, whereas <abbr title=\"relative deviation pooling\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">RDP</span></abbr> provides little benefit for EAT. These trends observed for <abbr title=\"relative deviation pooling\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">RDP</span></abbr> are consistent with the findings reported in <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib11\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">11</a>]</cite>.</p>\n</div>\n<div id=\"S4.SS2.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n<p id=\"S4.SS2.p3.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We also conducted an ablation study to evaluate the effect of applying <abbr title=\"low-rank adaptation\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">LoRA</span></abbr> to the NA layers during discriminative fine-tuning, as shown in <a href=\"#S4.T3\" title=\"In 4.2 Results ‣ 4 Experimental evaluation ‣ Anomalous Sound Detection Meets Noise-Aware Self-Supervised Learning\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">Table</span> <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">3</span></a>.\nAlthough discriminative fine-tuning remains effective even without <abbr title=\"low-rank adaptation\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">LoRA</span></abbr> in the NA layers, applying <abbr title=\"low-rank adaptation\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">LoRA</span></abbr> to the NA layers tends to yield better performance.</p>\n</div>\n<div id=\"S4.SS2.p4\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n<p id=\"S4.SS2.p4.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><a href=\"#S4.F3\" title=\"In 4.2 Results ‣ 4 Experimental evaluation ‣ Anomalous Sound Detection Meets Noise-Aware Self-Supervised Learning\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">Figure</span> <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">3</span></a> shows the results on the DCASE 2026 Challenge Task 2 evaluation dataset reported in <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib33\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">33</a>]</cite>.\nIt compares our Dis NA-BEATs with the ten top-performing systems <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib34\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">34</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib35\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">35</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib36\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">36</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib37\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">37</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib38\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">38</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib39\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">39</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib40\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">40</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib41\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">41</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib42\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">42</a>]</cite> and the official baseline system <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib7\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">7</a>]</cite>. Here, the backend consists of BEAM and <abbr title=\"relative deviation pooling\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">RDP</span></abbr> with <math id=\"S4.SS2.p4.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\gamma=4\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=4</annotation></semantics></math>, and the anomaly scores from three trials are ensembled.\nDis NA-BEATs ranked first on the evaluation dataset with an official score of 70.24%, surpassing the second-place system (65.46%) and the official baseline system (59.80%) by a considerable margin.\nIt is also noteworthy that our Dis NA-BEATs is built on only a single base <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> model with a single backend, whereas the other systems rely on ensembles of multiple <abbr title=\"self-supervised learning\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">SSL</span></abbr> models <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib35\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">35</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib40\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">40</a>]</cite> and multiple backends <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib35\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">35</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib38\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">38</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib39\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">39</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib40\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">40</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib42\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">42</a>]</cite>.\nMoreover, our Dis NA-BEATs outperforms the other BEATs-based systems <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib35\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">35</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib39\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">39</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib40\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">40</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib42\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">42</a>]</cite>.</p>\n</div>\n<figure id=\"S4.T3\" class=\"ltx_table\">\n<figcaption class=\"ltx_caption ltx_centering\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_table\">Table 3: </span>Ablation study of discriminative fine-tuning.\nThe values are harmonic mean of the official scores over machine types in the development dataset.\nThe values are reported as “Mean (CI95)”.\nThe backend consists of BEAM and <abbr title=\"relative deviation pooling\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">RDP</span></abbr> with <math id=\"S4.T3.2.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\gamma=4\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=4</annotation></semantics></math>.\nNA denotes <abbr title=\"noise-aware   ()\" class=\"ltx_glossaryref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_glossary_short\">NA-SSL</span></abbr> pre-training, and Dis denotes discriminative fine-tuning.\n</figcaption>\n<div id=\"S4.T3.3\" class=\"ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer\" style=\"width:433.6pt;height:61.7pt;vertical-align:-27.7pt;\"><span class=\"ltx_transformed_inner\" style=\"transform:translate(44.1pt,-6.3pt) scale(1.25505047081197,1.25505047081197) ;\">\n<table id=\"S4.T3.3.1\" class=\"ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle\">\n<tr id=\"S4.T3.3.1.1\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n<td id=\"S4.T3.3.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt\">Method</td>\n<td